<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Arzawa</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 1320 BCE (approx.) · capital Apasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Arzawa, the main kingdom of western Anatolia in the Late Bronze Age, a rival of the Hittites courted by Egypt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arzawa emerged by the 16th century BCE in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apasa">Apasa</a>, later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. Its Luwian-speaking rulers dominated the river valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> hinterland and exploited every period of Hittite weakness to expand eastward.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the 14th century BCE Arzawa was strong enough for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> to negotiate a marriage with the daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarhundaradu">Tarhundaradu</a>; the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> involved are written in Hittite. The kingdom had links with the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uhha-ziti">Uhha-ziti</a> c. 1320 BCE and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apasa">Apasa</a>; Uhha-ziti fled across the sea to the islands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> divided Arzawa into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mira">Mira</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seha-river-land">Seha River Land</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hapalla">Hapalla</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arzawa.jpg" alt="Arzawa" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Near_East_1400BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 1320 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tarhundaradu, Uhha-ziti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Arzawa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1410&amp;focus=arzawa" aria-label="Arzawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5534 760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arzawa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5852 1002h0M5679 1030h0M5777 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5854 1016h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5854 1016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-1410&amp;focus=arzawa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
